WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.820
Sometimes, films scare us…sometimes, they
move us to tears.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.340
And sometimes, they have us laughing our butts
off.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.120
There's a reason we love movies so much, and
it has everything to do with a well-crafted

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:11.620
scene.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:16.080
These are just a few cinematic scenes that
are practically perfect in every way.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:22.539
The 1996 slasher film Scream, just one of
the many horror classics conceived by Wes

00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:27.109
Craven, was at once a love letter to horror
films as well as an effective addition to

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:28.440
the genre in its own right.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.880
"No, please don't kill me Mr. Ghostface.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:32.720
I wanna be in the sequel!"

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.360
Set in a small town with a history of tragic
murders, the film centers on Sidney Prescott,

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.800
a seemingly normal teen who starts getting
mysterious phone calls as she and her friends

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.840
are stalked by an unknown entity known as
"Ghostface."

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:51.059
Before Sidney even appears, there is a lengthy
opening scene featuring Drew Barrymore as

00:00:51.059 --> 00:00:55.609
Casey Becker, a teenager enjoying a night
at home when she starts getting some unsettling

00:00:55.609 --> 00:00:56.340
phone calls.

00:00:56.609 --> 00:01:01.860
At first, Casey is unfazed by these odd calls,
assuming the person on the other end of the

00:01:01.871 --> 00:01:06.460
phone is simply playing a rude prank, but
before long, she starts getting nervous, and

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:10.660
once she sees her gutted boyfriend posed in
her backyard, her panic intensifies.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.560
As her parents arrive home at the end of the
evening, the camera zooms in on her body hanging

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:20.360
from a tree on the property, finally cutting
to the opening credits and capping off a perfect

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.620
encapsulation of both Craven's vision and
the horror genre.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:33.800
Despite initially flopping at the box office,
Frank Darabont's magnum opus The Shawshank

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:38.940
Redemption went on to become one of the most
beloved films of the 20th century, even earning

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.020
a spot on AFI's Top 100 list.

00:01:41.789 --> 00:01:47.120
Telling the story of Andy Dufresne, a wrongfully
imprisoned man serving two consecutive life

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:52.240
sentences at the Shawshank State Penitentiary,
the film paints the grimmest possible picture

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.060
of prison life.

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:57.799
Andy is brutalized every single day until
he figures out a scheme where he can help

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:02.300
financially manage the prison as well as lend
a hand to his fellow inmates with their money

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:02.800
troubles.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.700
The signature scene of the entire film is
the moment when Andy escapes from the prison

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.600
through a rock tunnel that took nearly 20
years to build.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:16.010
As he emerges from the tunnel, which is filled
with human waste and filth, he stands clean

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:21.260
and victorious in the rain, basking in a hard-fought
victory the audience feels right along with

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:21.760
him.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:35.880
It's a perfect moment of human triumph, and
Darabont created a completely unforgettable

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.980
image that will continue striking an emotional
chord in viewers for years to come.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.940
Adapting the Lord of the Rings series, comprised
of three novels by J.R.R.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:50.920
Tolkien, was no easy feat, but clearly one
man was up to the job: Peter Jackson, who

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:54.840
used his home country of New Zealand as the
stand-in for Tolkien's Middle-earth.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.880
The films were critical and commercial smashes,
with the final installment, The Return of

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.780
the King, winning a whopping 11 Oscars.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:08.680
In the first of the three films, The Fellowship
of the Ring, Frodo, a timid and inexperienced

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:14.190
hobbit of the Shire, embarks on his long journey
to destroy the One Ring that could control

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:14.940
the entire world.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.880
During his journey with the "fellowship" of
nine, made up of hobbits, men, elves, dwarves,

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.150
and wizards alike, the group needs to cross
through the dangerous Mines of Moria, home

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.200
to ancient monsters that lurk far underground.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:35.500
When they encounter a Balrog, an enormous
demon made of fire, all hope seems lost, until

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:40.330
the powerful wizard Gandalf sacrifices himself
to get Frodo and the rest of the Fellowship

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:40.980
to safety.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.220
"Fly, you fools!"

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:48.720
With still-impressive special effects, an
ideal action set piece, and strong performances

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.960
across the board, it's no wonder that…

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.320
"You shall not pass!"

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.060
…has become a part of the cultural lexicon.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:07.190
Thanks to director Quentin Tarantino's vision
of a non-linear gangster fairytale, 1994's

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:11.480
Pulp Fiction is regarded as one of the best
films of not just the 1990s, but the back

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.580
half of the 20th century.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.620
With an all-star cast that includes John Travolta,
Uma Thurman, Samuel L. Jackson, Ving Rhames,

00:04:19.629 --> 00:04:24.830
Bruce Willis, Christopher Walken, and Tim
Roth, the film follows several different stories

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:30.330
as they eventually converge, with the entire
plot happening out of order as well as beginning

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.580
and ending at the exact same point during
a diner robbery.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:39.560
Though the film is filled with plenty of unforgettable
scenes, the most memorable is arguably Mia

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.140
Wallace's overdose and the aftermath that
follows.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.620
Wallace, played by Thurman, misidentifies
Vega's drugs after an evening out, and promptly

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:48.560
snorts them.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:54.160
After rushing an unconscious Mia to his dealer's
house, Vincent ends up stabbing her in the

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.700
heart with an adrenaline-filled hypodermic
needle to revive her.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:12.139
Between the ensuing insanity that takes place
over Mia's still body and Thurman's reaction

00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:16.300
to the needle, this scene is shot and written
so perfectly that you almost feel like you're

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.100
a part of the action.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.720
"If you're alright, then say something."

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:22.680
"Something."

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:28.280
A great opening can perfectly set the tone
for a film, and few are as famous as the sequence

00:05:28.289 --> 00:05:33.060
that opens Steven Spielberg's Jaws, the classic
horror movie that made millions of people

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.500
suddenly reconsider taking a dip in the ocean.

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:40.020
The film itself, which is considered one of
the best movies of all time, starred Richard

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:45.900
Dreyfuss and Roy Scheider as a marine biologist
and police chief trying to take down a man-eating

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.970
great white shark that is terrorizing a resort
town in New England, and though they're ultimately

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:54.080
successful, the shark takes several lives
in the process.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:06:00.560
Thanks to an often unreliable mechanical shark
on set, Spielberg ended up not showing his

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:05.310
toothy antagonist in many scenes, creating
a Hitchcock-esque thriller where the main

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.320
villain's mere presence evoked fear.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.879
This is perfectly displayed in the opening
scene: After a party on the beach, a young

00:06:11.879 --> 00:06:15.770
girl and her boyfriend take a swim during
which they are both attacked and the girl

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:16.880
is eaten by the shark.

00:06:17.409 --> 00:06:22.160
By filming crucial parts of the scene from
the shark's perspective and using menacing,

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:27.469
and now iconic, music by John Williams, Spielberg
takes a "less is more" approach to creating

00:06:27.469 --> 00:06:28.740
this unforgettable scene.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:35.860
One of David Fincher's most highly regarded
films, 1995's Se7en is a classically dark

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:40.060
and twisted thriller from the acclaimed director,
as well as one of his first big hits.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:45.340
Starring Morgan Freeman and Brad Pitt, the
film centers on two detectives, Mills and

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:50.129
Somerset, tracking a violent and creative
serial killer who taunts police by leaving

00:06:50.129 --> 00:06:54.560
his victims in deliberate arrangements meant
to evoke the seven deadly sins.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:07:00.000
With each crime carefully covered up and meticulously
planned, the detectives have no leads until

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.720
John Doe arrives at the police station covered
in blood to confess.

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:08.960
Doe then leads both detectives to a remote
spot in the desert to show them his final

00:07:08.979 --> 00:07:13.360
crime, revealing that he has been guilty of
the sin of envy, leading him to murder Mills'

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.970
pregnant wife Tracy, and presenting Mills
with Tracy's head in a box.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:19.940
"Because I envy your normal life…"

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:20.640
"Put the gun down, David."

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.520
"...it seems that envy is my sin."

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.140
"No, what's in the box?"

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:25.540
"Not 'til you give me the gun."

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:26.760
"What's in the f---ing box?!"

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:32.240
In response, an enraged Mills shoots Doe,
representing wrath and doing exactly what

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:33.140
Doe wants.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:38.720
The scene, with its sepia-toned color scheme,
tense music, and impeccable performances,

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:43.759
is a master class in thrillers, especially
since the audience never even sees the contents

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:44.420
of the box.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:45.540
We don't need to.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.560
In fact, the scene is better, and more gut-wrenching,
with just the mental image.

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:57.379
The final moments of The Usual Suspects, the
1995 neo-noir mystery film starring Kevin

00:07:57.379 --> 00:08:02.659
Spacey as Verbal Kint, one of the sole survivors
of a shootout led by a notorious crime boss

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:07.440
named Keyser Soze, are regarded as one of
the best twist endings in film history.

00:08:07.909 --> 00:08:13.060
The script focuses almost entirely on Kint's
recollection of the events preceding the film,

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:16.729
which involves a group of gangsters attempting
to pull off a complicated heist in order to

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:22.650
get revenge on the NYPD, only to be foiled
by Soze, who seems more like an urban legend

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:23.600
than a real person.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.480
As the detectives frantically try to figure
out which person in the story could possibly

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:34.660
be Soze, Kint's bail is posted, and he leaves
the police station…and as he does, his physicality

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:39.820
completely changes; his limp goes away, and
his non-functioning hand suddenly works.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.040
Back at the station, the detectives realize
that Kint was Keyser Soze the entire time,

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:47.920
but they're too late, he's already escaped.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:52.960
"The greatest trick the devil ever pulled
was convincing the world he didn't exist."

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:58.540
No matter how many times you've seen the film,
the ending still completely works, especially

00:08:58.540 --> 00:09:03.350
once you can look back at all the clues the
film laid throughout to lead viewers to solve

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:04.350
the mystery themselves.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:11.240
Christopher Nolan rebooted the Batman franchise
to rave reviews in 2005 with Batman Begins,

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:15.960
but that was nothing compared to 2008's The
Dark Knight, widely regarded as one of the

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:20.700
best films of the decade, as well as the gold
standard that future superhero movies would

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:21.360
strive for.

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:27.100
Nolan cast Heath Ledger, then mainly known
for arthouse dramas and teenage romantic comedies,

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.580
as the Joker to play opposite Christian Bale's
Batman.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:34.560
And, though the casting was initially controversial,
the bet paid off handsomely.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:40.180
Tragically, Ledger passed away in January
of 2008 before the film's summer release,

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:43.270
but his performance in the film remains one
of the best of all time.

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:47.440
"See, madness, as you know, is like gravity.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:50.840
All it takes it a little push!"

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:56.120
There are almost too many incredible scenes
featuring Ledger to narrow them down to one,

00:09:56.220 --> 00:10:01.380
but one of his best occurs when he crashes
a high society gala to torment Harvey Dent,

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:05.540
terrorizing guests and throwing glasses of
champagne with reckless abandon.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:10.960
The scene is a perfect encapsulation of chaos
on any scale, whether that means committing

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:16.280
murder or simply making a mess as the Joker
traverses the party, cackling as he eats off

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:19.220
random trays and explains his disfigured face.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:21.180
"Now, I'm always smiling!

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:27.580
A little fight in you, I like that."

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:32.440
What's left to say about the original Star
Wars, one of the most influential and beloved

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:33.440
films of all time?

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:38.920
The story of Luke Skywalker, a young Jedi
who must confront the Galactic Empire alongside

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:42.840
the virtuous Rebel Alliance, is one of the
most important movies ever made.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:47.900
Its success spawned a universe of sequels
and spinoffs, creating an entire industry.

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:53.080
The first film released in the series, A New
Hope, made worldwide stars out of Mark Hamill,

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:56.820
who played Skywalker, along with co-stars
Harrison Ford and Carrie Fisher.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:02.260
The film's final battle scene, the trench
run at the Death Star, is among the most famous

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:07.620
in a film arguably filled with iconic moments,
and it's easy to see why: Director George

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:13.110
Lucas and his crew used mostly practical effects
to create a stunning 15-minute sequence during

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:18.100
which Luke and a squad of Alliance pilots
attack the Death Star, eventually blowing

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:21.560
the fortress-like ship to smithereens by bombing
an exhaust port.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:25.900
Tense, thrilling, and victorious, it's Star
Wars at its very best.

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:30.980
One of the most famous directors in history,
Alfred Hitchcock is responsible for some of

00:11:30.981 --> 00:11:36.210
the most famous and unforgettable scenes in
all of film, arguably led by that infamous

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:37.660
shower scene in Psycho.

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:43.420
As Marion Crane, a young woman who stops at
the Bates Motel, Janet Leigh seems to be the

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:48.590
hero of the story, but in a perfect subversion
of expectations, Marion is stabbed to death

00:11:48.590 --> 00:11:51.940
by an unseen attacker while taking a shower
at the motel.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:02.920
The scene, which has no score or music until
the murder, focuses tightly on Leigh, which

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:07.510
only increases the audience's sense of dread
when they see a shadowy figure through the

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:08.120
shower curtain.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:14.580
The camera then lingers on Leigh's body, eventually
contrasting her open eye with the open drain,

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:16.360
allowing for a sense of symmetry.

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:22.600
This three-minute scene, which contains only
45 seconds of action, remains one of the gold

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:28.220
standards of the horror genre, so much so
that an entire documentary about it, 78/52,

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:33.880
was released in 2017, offering viewers a whole
new insight into this masterful cinematic

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:34.380
moment.

